  "configuration_id": "config_004",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_001 (RNA-only, singleprofile, PCA-2000) achieved 48.5% acc, while config_010 (image-only, ResNet50) achieved only 20.8% acc. The RNA modality is clearly the stronger signal, but we have no evidence yet on whether the PCA representation is near its ceiling or whether a learned nonlinear encoder could substantially improve it. Question: config_004 (RNA-only, singleprofile, scvi_n200) is a single-variable change from config_001—only the RNA encoder shifts from linear PCA to a learned variational autoencoder—so it cleanly isolates the effect of representation quality. Why this is the best next step: (1) It directly tests whether the 48.5% baseline is a representation limitation or a data limitation, which fundamentally shapes all downstream decisions (multiprofile, fusion, encoder choice). (2) If scvi substantially outperforms PCA, we gain a strong prior for using learned encoders in multiprofile and multimodal settings, making later experiments more targeted. If it does not, we learn that the RNA signal is inherently limited under these conditions and can redirect budget toward aggregation or fusion. (3) It avoids the confound of simultaneously changing encoder, profile setting, and aggregation, keeping the comparison interpretable under a tight budget."
}

